WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:03.650
Re-routing a branch breaks things
for a short list of reasons,

2
00:00:03.650 --> 00:00:05.162
so design around the list.

3
00:00:05.162 --> 00:00:06.951
Proxies reap idle connections,

4
00:00:06.951 --> 00:00:11.491
TLS inspection breaks pinned certificates
and channel-bound authentication,

5
00:00:11.491 --> 00:00:16.375
and a change in the client's apparent source
address resets established connections.

6
00:00:16.375 --> 00:00:19.975
A path change doesn't
break TCP sequence numbers.

7
00:00:19.975 --> 00:00:23.113
Address continuity is the
property that matters.

8
00:00:23.113 --> 00:00:26.230
Kerberos tolerates NAT
under Windows defaults:

9
00:00:26.230 --> 00:00:31.138
the domain controller doesn't put client
addresses in tickets or check them.

10
00:00:31.138 --> 00:00:33.866
And there's no
five-millisecond requirement;

11
00:00:33.866 --> 00:00:36.374
the five is minutes of allowed clock skew.

12
00:00:36.374 --> 00:00:41.914
What genuinely needs a reachable domain
controller is short: initial logon, service

13
00:00:41.914 --> 00:00:47.137
tickets for on-premises services, Group
Policy, password change and hybrid join.

14
00:00:47.137 --> 00:00:51.360
Leave that path out and everything
works except signing in,

15
00:00:51.360 --> 00:00:54.375
while Kerberos quietly falls back to NTLM.

16
00:00:54.375 --> 00:00:57.150
Round trips dominate chatty applications.

17
00:00:57.150 --> 00:01:02.603
In the book's illustrative arithmetic, a
better branch path saves 17 to 25 percent;

18
00:01:02.603 --> 00:01:07.870
cutting 30 sequential round trips to
three saves about an order of magnitude.
